    What is a Pod?    A Pod is the smallest deployable unit in Kubernetes. It contains one or more containers.
1. Pod
File: pod.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod

metadata:
  name: nginx-pod

spec:
  containers:
    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:latest
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80

Interview answer    "A Pod is the smallest unit in Kubernetes. It provides an environment in which one or more containers run.
Usually, we run one main application container per Pod."


One Pod with 2 containers
apiVersion: v1
kind: Pod

metadata:
  name: my-pod

spec:
  containers:

    - name: app
      image: nginx

    - name: sidecar
      image: busybox
      command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo hello; sleep 10; done"]


The BusyBox container doesn't automatically provide a web application like Nginx. It is often used for:
Testing network connectivity
Running small scripts
Debugging
Performing helper tasks
Acting as a simple sidecar container

For example, it can test whether another service is reachable:
wget http://my-service
In our previous example

We used:
image: busybox
command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo hello; sleep 10; done"]

So that particular BusyBox container simply:
Starts
 ↓
prints "hello"
 ↓
waits 10 seconds
 ↓
prints "hello"
 ↓
repeats

It was only an example of a second container. In a real application, the second container would normally perform some useful helper/sidecar function.



If you mean why we use this command with BusyBox:
command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo hello; sleep 10; done"]

We use it to keep the BusyBox container running.

Why?
If you write only:
- name: sidecar
  image: busybox

BusyBox starts, executes its default command, and then exits.
But we want the second container to stay alive inside the Pod:

Pod
├── nginx container      → keeps running
└── busybox container    → should keep running

So we give BusyBox a continuous command:
while true
   ↓
echo hello
   ↓
sleep 10
   ↓
repeat forever

Therefore:   command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo hello; sleep 10; done"]
means:  "Start a shell and keep running forever, printing hello every 10 seconds."

But in a real application
You don't normally add this just because there are 2 containers.
If your Docker image already has a proper long-running command, you can simply do:
containers:
  - name: app
    image: myapp:latest

  - name: sidecar
    image: mysidecar:latest

No command is needed.
So the busybox example was mainly to demonstrate two containers in one Pod.


So remember:   command is not specifically for BusyBox. It is used when you want to override the image's default command or specify what the container should execute.

not only BusyBox. The command depends on the container image and what you want the container to do.
  image: nginx:latest
No command is needed because the Nginx image already has a default command that keeps Nginx running.
    command: ["sh", "-c", "while true; do echo hello; sleep 10; done"]
We use command here because the default BusyBox command doesn't keep the container running.

spec:
  containers:

    - name: nginx
      image: nginx:latest
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80

    - name: httpd
      image: httpd:latest
      ports:
        - containerPort: 80
