“Metrics Server is not guaranteed to be installed when we create a Kubernetes cluster. If it is not available, 
we install it separately to collect CPU and memory metrics for kubectl top and HPA.”

Install Metrics Server   kubectl apply -f https://github.com/kubernetes-sigs/metrics-server/releases/latest/download/components.yaml
Check whether it is running     kubectl get pods -n kube-system
Look for something like:     metrics-server-xxxxx    1/1    Running


It means Metrics Server is a Kubernetes component that collects CPU and memory usage of your Pods and Nodes.
Without it:  kubectl top pods
may not work because Kubernetes doesn't have the current usage data.


If you mean Metrics Server: it is not always installed by default.
In many Kubernetes clusters, you need to install Metrics Server separately.
Some managed Kubernetes setups or local tools may already include/configure it.
Check with:  kubectl top pods

If it shows CPU and memory usage → Metrics Server is working.
If you get an error like:  error: Metrics API not available
→ Metrics Server is not available/configured.



2.Set CPU for a Pod

In your deployment.yaml:
resources:
  requests:
    cpu: "250m"
  limits:
    cpu: "500m"

Full example:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: my-app

spec:
  replicas: 2

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:latest

          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: "250m"
              memory: "128Mi"

            limits:
              cpu: "500m"
              memory: "256Mi"


What is requests?
requests:
  cpu: "250m"

means:  Kubernetes should reserve 250m CPU for this container when scheduling it.
The scheduler looks at the request when deciding which Worker Node has enough resources.

Worker Node
    |
    ├── Pod A → requests 250m
    ├── Pod B → requests 500m
    └── Pod C → requests 250m
What is limits?
limits:
  cpu: "500m"

means:  The container can use up to 500m CPU.
So:
Request = 250m
Limit   = 500m

If traffic increases, the application may need more CPU.
It can use more CPU, because the limit is 500m.

250m request
      ↓
Traffic increases
      ↓
CPU can increase
      ↓
400m
      ↓
up to 500m
What if traffic becomes VERY high?
Suppose it needs:  800m CPU
but you configured:  limit: 500m
The container cannot use more than its CPU limit. CPU gets throttled.

This is where HPA (Horizontal Pod Autoscaler) becomes useful.

For example:
Low traffic:
1 Pod

High traffic:
3 Pods

Very high traffic:
5 Pods

So there are two different ways to handle increased traffic:

Vertical capacity of one container:  250m → 400m → 500m
Horizontal scaling with HPA:   1 Pod → 2 Pods → 3 Pods



If you mean how to configure HPA so that Pods increase from 1 → 3 → 5 when traffic/CPU increases, you do it with an HPA YAML file.

1. Deployment
First, create a Deployment with resource requests:

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment

metadata:
  name: my-app

spec:
  replicas: 1

  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: my-app

  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: my-app

    spec:
      containers:
        - name: nginx
          image: nginx:latest

          resources:
            requests:
              cpu: "250m"
            limits:
              cpu: "500m"

The important part for HPA is:

requests:
  cpu: "250m"

2. HPA file

Create:
hpa.yaml
apiVersion: autoscaling/v2
kind: HorizontalPodAutoscaler

metadata:
  name: my-app-hpa

spec:
  scaleTargetRef:
    apiVersion: apps/v1
    kind: Deployment
    name: my-app

  minReplicas: 1
  maxReplicas: 5

  metrics:
    - type: Resource
      resource:
        name: cpu
        target:
          type: Utilization
          averageUtilization: 70

Apply both:
kubectl apply -f deployment.yaml
kubectl apply -f hpa.yaml
How does it work?

You configured:
minReplicas = 1
maxReplicas = 5
target CPU = 70%

So Kubernetes can do:
Low traffic
     ↓
CPU < 70%
     ↓
1 Pod

Traffic increases:
CPU > 70%
     ↓
HPA
     ↓
2 Pods
     ↓
3 Pods

More traffic:
CPU still high
     ↓
HPA
     ↓
4 Pods
     ↓
5 Pods

It can scale up to 5 Pods, but HPA doesn't literally mean "1 → 3 → 5" at fixed traffic levels. It calculates the desired replica count based on the current metrics.

Check HPA
kubectl get hpa

Example:
NAME         REFERENCE           TARGETS   MINPODS   MAXPODS   REPLICAS
my-app-hpa   Deployment/my-app   80%/70%   1         5         3

Here:
80% = current CPU utilization
70% = target
3   = current number of Pods

